

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good — that settles the architecture. One consequence worth naming up front: with in-page delivery, a notification can
  only fire while the app is open in a tab. That's fine, but it means the design has to answer "what about reminders
  that came due while you were away," which I'll get to.

  First, a scope question I can't skip. Since no tasks exist, this spec has to define the task model itself — reminders
  are a property of tasks, so the shape of a task is part of the notification design, not separate from it.

  [1mThe fork:[0m how much task the model carries. This is the one decision here that's genuinely expensive to change later,
  because it's the stored format in localStorage — changing it after real data exists means writing a migration.

  - [1mMinimal[0m — [38;5;153mid[39m, [38;5;153mtitle[39m, [38;5;153mdueAt[39m, [38;5;153mdone[39m. Everything the reminder system needs and nothing else. Cheapest to build, and
    keeps the spec honestly about notifications. Rules out per-task reminder customization until you extend it.
  - [1mMinimal + per-task reminder settings[0m — the above plus a [38;5;153mremindAt[39m or lead-time override per task. Buys "remind me 1
    day before this one, 10 minutes before that one." Costs one more field and some UI.
  - [1mFuller[0m — notes, priority, tags, subtasks. Makes this a real task app, but the notification work becomes a small part
    of a much larger build, and most of those fields don't affect reminders at all.

  [1mMy recommendation:[0m minimal. The clock is the only change source, so [38;5;153mdueAt[39m is the only field notifications actually
  read. A global default lead time ("remind me 30 minutes before") covers most of what per-task settings buy, at a
  fraction of the UI. Adding [38;5;153mremindAt[39m later is an additive field — that's the one migration that stays cheap.
